ERROR REPORTS

Please send errors and suggestions to Frans Kaashoek and Robert Morris
(kaashoek,rtm@mit.edu).  The main purpose of xv6 is as a teaching
operating system for MIT's 6.1810, so we are more interested in
simplifications and clarifications than new features.

BUILDING AND RUNNING XV6

You will need a RISC-V "newlib" tool chain from
https://github.com/riscv/riscv-gnu-toolchain, and qemu compiled for
riscv64-softmmu.  Once they are installed, and in your shell
search path, you can run "make qemu".

PROPOSED FEATURES AND ENHANCEMENTS

The following features are proposed enhancements for this xv6 project.
They are planned kernel-level additions and should not be considered
implemented until they have been developed and tested.

1. Process Information System 
   Add functionality to display process
   information, including process ID (PID), parent process ID (PPID),
   process state, and memory usage.

2. Priority-Based CPU Scheduling 
   Extend the process scheduler to
   support process priorities, allowing the scheduler to select
   processes based on their assigned priority.
3. Process Execution Statistics
   Track process execution information,
   including CPU ticks, execution time, and scheduling activity.

4. Kernel Memory Statistics 
   Provide information about physical memory,
   including total memory, allocated memory, and available memory.

5. Custom System Calls 
   Introduce additional system calls to allow user
   programs to request selected kernel-level information and services.

6. Process Limit Management
   Add a configurable limit on the number of
   processes that can exist or run simultaneously in the system.

7. Semaphore Synchronization
   Implement semaphore-based synchronization
   to coordinate processes and control access to shared resources.

8. Kernel Event Logging
   Maintain a kernel event log containing
   important events, such as process creation, process termination, and
   selected kernel activities.

9. CPU Core Information 
   Provide information about the available CPU
   cores and their basic configuration in the RISC-V multiprocessor
   environment.

10. Process Resource Monitoring
    Add monitoring of per-process resource
    usage, including CPU utilization information and memory allocation

